WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.380
In the tank, oxygen rose by day and fell at night.

2
00:00:04.720 --> 00:00:09.660
Why? Picture a whole creek sharing the two jobs from the first film.

3
00:00:10.940 --> 00:00:14.380
Keep the same colors while the view pulls back into moving water.

4
00:00:17.867 --> 00:00:21.327
Green algae and underwater grass fill the sunlit creek.

5
00:00:22.547 --> 00:00:25.467
Fish, tiny animals, and microbes drift nearby.

6
00:00:26.787 --> 00:00:31.327
A blue gauge tracks dissolved oxygen, the oxygen gas mixed into water.

7
00:00:32.567 --> 00:00:34.247
A time bar begins at sunrise.

8
00:00:35.417 --> 00:00:38.057
Yellow sunlight reaches the algae and grass.

9
00:00:39.397 --> 00:00:41.717
These producers make food by photosynthesis.

10
00:00:43.057 --> 00:00:46.797
Their photosynthesis sends blue oxygen bubbles into the water.

11
00:00:48.077 --> 00:00:52.537
At the same time, red respiration arrows pull oxygen toward living things.

12
00:00:53.837 --> 00:00:55.217
Both processes are running.

13
00:00:56.300 --> 00:00:58.480
Watch the arrows, not just the bubbles.

14
00:00:59.740 --> 00:01:01.820
A rate tells how fast a process runs.

15
00:01:03.120 --> 00:01:06.780
The photosynthesis arrow grows wider than the respiration arrow.

16
00:01:08.160 --> 00:01:11.700
Oxygen enters the water faster than living things use it.

17
00:01:12.700 --> 00:01:15.580
The blue gauge climbs, and the schematic line rises.

18
00:01:16.783 --> 00:01:21.103
The grass and algae appear beside animals on the respiration side.

19
00:01:22.483 --> 00:01:25.503
Plants do not stop respiring when they photosynthesize.

20
00:01:26.683 --> 00:01:28.923
Their cells also need usable energy.

21
00:01:30.303 --> 00:01:35.183
During daylight, whether oxygen rises or falls depends on which rate is faster.

22
00:01:36.267 --> 00:01:38.007
The time bar reaches sunset.

23
00:01:39.307 --> 00:01:43.187
The yellow light fades, so photosynthesis stops adding new oxygen.

24
00:01:44.667 --> 00:01:46.407
Respiration keeps using oxygen.

25
00:01:47.707 --> 00:01:50.807
If nothing else changes, what should the blue line do next?

26
00:01:56.750 --> 00:01:58.930
The line bends downward through the night.

27
00:02:00.150 --> 00:02:03.310
Fish, plants, and microbes keep respiring in darkness.

28
00:02:04.630 --> 00:02:10.710
The oxygen gauge falls because living things keep using oxygen and photosynthesis no

29
00:02:10.710 --> 00:02:11.930
longer replaces it.

30
00:02:13.190 --> 00:02:18.210
Photosynthesis adds oxygen in light; respiration uses oxygen day and night.

31
00:02:19.450 --> 00:02:22.650
At sunrise, light can turn the green addition back on.

32
00:02:23.683 --> 00:02:26.523
Transform the creek gauge into a bathtub level.

33
00:02:27.883 --> 00:02:30.263
Photosynthesis is a tap controlled by light.

34
00:02:31.663 --> 00:02:33.843
Respiration is a drain that stays open.

35
00:02:35.163 --> 00:02:37.683
When the tap outruns the drain, the level rises.

36
00:02:39.143 --> 00:02:40.283
With the tap off, it falls.

37
00:02:41.383 --> 00:02:43.783
Now transform the tub back into the creek.

38
00:02:45.123 --> 00:02:48.123
Make both rate arrows equal, and the gauge holds steady.

39
00:02:49.603 --> 00:02:51.863
Widen photosynthesis, and the gauge rises.

40
00:02:53.463 --> 00:02:55.083
Widen respiration, and it falls.

41
00:02:56.463 --> 00:03:00.503
Whether the level rises or falls follows the difference between the rates.

42
00:03:01.583 --> 00:03:05.343
The smooth line is a model, not a promise about every hour.

43
00:03:06.603 --> 00:03:09.023
A real sensor may not follow it exactly.

44
00:03:10.443 --> 00:03:14.063
Use the model to ask which process pushes oxygen up or down.

45
00:03:15.343 --> 00:03:17.503
Then investigate other causes separately.

46
00:03:18.683 --> 00:03:22.823
Compare the green addition with the red use before judging the line.

47
00:03:24.063 --> 00:03:25.563
In light, both arrows run.

48
00:03:26.803 --> 00:03:29.643
In darkness, the use continues without the addition.
